  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 308 AM CDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nStrong and persistent west-northwest flow continues Tuesday and into \nlate week, with the daily threat for showers and thunderstorms \ncontinuing as well. This strong flow aloft combined with dewpoints \nin the middle to upper 60s leads to strongly sheared and at least \nmoderately unstable conditions across the area each afternoon into \nlate week. Confidence is highest in convection on Tuesday afternoon \nand evening, as a cold front begins to push across the area from \nnorth to south. This surface front looks to provide a focus for \nscattered thunderstorm development, and the overlap of favorable \ndeep layer shear and instability points to an initial supercellular \nstorm mode. These initial discrete/semi-discrete updrafts will pose \na risk for large hail, before a propensity for upscale growth along \nthe encroaching cold front leads to the damaging wind threat \nincreasing with time. \n\nAdditional strong to severe storms appear possible nearly each day \nas we head into late week, though mesoscale details remain unclear \nand will drive the coverage and magnitude of any threat each day. \nTrends will continue to be monitored with respect to this. By \nFriday, upper ridging will begin to amplify across the Rockies and \nbegin to slowly move east into the Plains into this weekend. This \nwill lead to temperatures warming into the upper 80s to lower 90s, \nalong with lessening precipitation chances across the area.",
